Choosing a château wedding venue in the Loire Valley means picking between three hundred estates that all look alike in photographs. The images are much of a muchness, and so are the brochures. What really sets them apart shows up on a site visit and on the phone: how many guests the place truly holds, whether you will have it to yourselves, where your closest family will sleep, and what happens if it rains.

Here are the criteria I see weighing on the couples we welcome at La Huberdière, in the Touraine near Amboise, and the questions worth asking before you sign. This guide is about choosing the venue. Planning the day itself is another matter.

## How do you choose a château for your wedding?

Six criteria almost always decide the final choice, and the charm of the building only comes sixth. In the order that matters:

- **Real seated capacity**, both sitting and standing, not the theoretical capacity of the grounds
- **Exclusivity**: will you be the only ones on the estate that day, or will you share it
- **Beds on site**, which decide how the end of the evening goes
- **Freedom of suppliers**: caterer imposed or free, corkage charged or not
- **The wet weather plan**, a proper room rather than a marquee hired at the last minute
- **Your guests' journey**, in hours on the road and in stations served

The rest, the fine stonework, the mature trees, the photogenic staircase, can be found anywhere in the Loire Valley. The region holds the densest concentration of historic houses in France, listed as a UNESCO World Heritage site since 2000 between Sully-sur-Loire and Chalonnes. You will have no trouble finding beautiful. You will have trouble finding practical.

## How many guests can a wedding château hold?

The capacity that counts is the seated dinner, not the drinks reception: that is what caps your guest list. Allow 1.5 to 2 m² per guest for a seated meal with a dance floor, which means 200 m² at the very least for 100 people.

![Vaulted reception hall laid for a wedding, round tables in white linen and gilded chairs with burgundy seats](/images/inline/mariage/mariage-08.webp "Round tables of ten seat more guests per square metre than long tables, and they leave the dance floor clear.")

La Huberdière hosts 30 to 150 guests. Dinner is served in the great vaulted hall, drinks on the terrace or in the grounds, and the Orangery gives you a second covered space. Four drawing rooms round things off, for the children, the speeches, or the guests who want to sit somewhere quiet.

Above 150 people, this is no longer the right estate, and I would rather say so on the first call than at the quotation stage. A wedding of 250 guests in a venue built for 150 is paid for in queues at the bar and a dinner served at two in the morning. Always ask the owner about the largest reception they have actually hosted, not the figure printed in the brochure.

## Is exclusive hire really exclusive?

This is the question that separates two business models, and many couples find out too late. Some châteaux let a reception room while keeping their hotel rooms or their tours running alongside: you end up passing guests in swimming costumes during your drinks reception.

At La Huberdière, exclusive hire covers the whole estate. The château, the fourteen hectares of walled grounds, the lake and the pool are yours and your guests', with no other event at the same time. We host one wedding at a time, and that deliberately limits our calendar.

Three questions to ask, word for word: are there any other bookings that weekend, what time must we leave the premises the next day, and are the grounds open to the public. The answers will tell you more than the visit.

## How much does it cost to hire a château in the Loire Valley for a wedding?

Hiring a château for a wedding in the Loire Valley usually costs between 4,000 and 8,000 € for a weekend, when the equivalent near Paris asks 10,000 to 15,000 €. The gap comes from demand around the capital, not from the character of the venues.

At La Huberdière, exclusive hire from Friday to Sunday runs from 4,000 to 8,000 € depending on the season, with all 21 beds included. Reception furniture is added at 15 € per person, tables and chairs included, which saves hiring it in. And you bring your own wines and champagnes with no corkage: on that line, a caterer's invoice is rarely kind.

Be wary of quotations that look low. Check what is included and what is not: furniture, crockery, final clean, extra hours after two in the morning, accommodation. Our article on the [cost of a château wedding](/en/blog/chateau-wedding-cost) breaks down every item, catering included.

## Where do the guests sleep on the wedding night?

Beds on site change the end of the evening more than any other criterion: without them, the party stops when the last taxi leaves. An estate in the Indre-et-Loire that houses your nearest and dearest spares you the shuttles, the six o'clock alarms and the hotel negotiations for thirty people.

![Wedding dress hanging from an antique wardrobe in a château bedroom, pale panelling and window light](/images/inline/mariage/mariage-04.webp "Getting ready happens on site that same morning, which removes the drive in the dress and an hour of stress.")

The château has 10 bedrooms, sleeping 21, spread through the house. Close family and witnesses stay there, while the other guests find rooms in Amboise and Nazelles-Négron, a few minutes down the road. Twenty one people is not many set against 150 guests: ask for the exact number of beds, never the number of rooms, as the difference is often twofold.

For guests who want to linger, our [charming rooms](/en/boutique-hotel-amboise) stay open on the Sunday night, and our guide to [visiting the Loire châteaux](/en/blog/visiting-loire-chateaux) gives them enough to turn Monday into a proper holiday.

## Can you marry outdoors, and what happens if it rains?

An outdoor wedding is only worth planning with a covered plan B, booked and sized for the same number of guests. That is the only weather question that matters, and many estates answer it with a marquee to hire at extra cost.

![Humanist ceremony on the château's gravel terrace, guests seated on wrought iron chairs facing blue and white hydrangeas](/images/inline/mariage/mariage-02.webp "The terrace hosts the ceremony facing the grounds, with the great vaulted hall thirty seconds' walk away as a back-up.")

Here, the humanist ceremony takes place under the century old trees or on the terrace, looking out over the valley. If it rains, the great vaulted hall and the Orangery take over at no extra charge and with no lorry to call in. The grounds are walled, which counts when children are running everywhere during the drinks reception.

One point that avoids misunderstandings: the civil ceremony is held at the town hall, in Nazelles-Négron or in your own commune. The grounds host the humanist ceremony, which carries no legal weight but is often the most moving moment of the day.

## How far from Paris can you marry in the Loire Valley?

Allow 2 hrs 20 by road from Paris to the Touraine, or an hour by TGV to Saint-Pierre-des-Corps followed by a 30 minute drive. That is the threshold that decides whether a guest comes, and whether they arrive in good spirits.

This distance is a real selection criterion, not a logistical detail. Under an hour from Paris, you pay capital prices. Beyond four hours, some of your guests decline or leave before dessert. The Loire Valley sits in the middle, with the bonus of stations serving Amboise and Tours.

Tell your guests early and put the train times in the invitation: that is the touch that lifts the acceptance rate. For the rest of the planning, our guide to [organising a château wedding](/en/blog/chateau-wedding-planning) covers the full sequence, from the countdown to the day after.

## Which season should you choose, and how far ahead should you book?

Saturdays in June and September go 18 months ahead, while the rest of the year books 8 to 12 months out. This is the constraint that governs your choice of venue most: the finest château in the Loire Valley is no use to you if it is taken on your date.

Two levers open doors. The day first: a Friday or a Sunday frees up dates and brings the hire rate down. The season next: from October to March the light comes in low between the great trees and hire prices fall across the region. We are open all year, and our article on a [winter wedding at the château](/en/blog/winter-wedding-chateau-loire) sets out what that season really changes, for the styling as much as for the budget.

My advice, after several wedding seasons here: settle the venue first, then the date. Doing it the other way round forces you into a venue by default, and it shows in the photographs.

Tell us your date, your guest numbers and the spirit you have in mind to [plan your wedding at the château](/en/chateau-wedding-loire): I will send you a costed proposal within 24 hours.
